#   seed 1            verdict=FAIL     GEN_UT_LOCKSTEP retired 176 consumed 175 mismatches 0 tohost 0x00000001 AssertionError: GEN_UT_LOCKSTEP: comparator consumed 175 records, 176 retired (must be equal)
#   seed 391581543    verdict=FAIL     GEN_UT_LOCKSTEP retired 177 consumed 176 mismatches 0 tohost 0x00000001 AssertionError: GEN_UT_LOCKSTEP: comparator consumed 176 records, 177 retired (must be equal)
#   SWEEP DONE lockstep prefix=mlkA_ seeds=2 2026-09-04T20:56:06Z
#   == MUT-RETSKEW1 build, FIXED intg_store: expect FAIL
#   seed 2062654708   verdict=FAIL      
#   SWEEP DONE intg_store prefix=mstA_ seeds=1 2026-09-04T20:56:30Z
# $ command grep -a 'AssertionError' $S/l33/l40_mut_root/outC/mspA_3/stdout.log | tail -1   [the intg_span mutant run]
#   AssertionError: GEN_EXPORT: marker records 30 != retired 31 (the sink and the bridge disagree)
# READ IT AS IT IS. On the fixed LOCKSTEP the pair assertion itself reports the gap at both seeds, consumed one
# short of retired (175 against 176, 176 against 177): the bounded loop ran out and the test failed, which is the
# claim. On the fixed intg_span and intg_store the same mutation is caught EARLIER, by the export sink's own
# cross-check of marker records against the bridge's retired count, so those two runs never reach the pair
# assertion and are NOT evidence about the loop. They are reported here rather than omitted.
# The predicate is BYTE-IDENTICAL in the three files (one tool inserts one block), so the behaviour proven on
# lockstep is the behaviour of all three call sites. Deliberate repetition, not a shared helper: the ruling scoped
# this landing to the three tests, and a helper on the bridge object would touch every test that imports it. That
# helper is the follow-up if a fourth site ever needs the same predicate.
#
# ================ THE CONTROL ================
# The two lockstep seeds that fail on the mutated build pass on the clean build with the same fixed test: seeds
# 391581543 and 1 in the green block above (lkB_). So the mutation, not the fix, is what those failures show.
#
# ================ THE RED FIXTURE ON THE CHANGED MODULE STILL FIRES ================
# Forty testlist entries name dv.auto_dv.gen_tb.gen_tests.gen_ut_lockstep, one of them the red fixture
# gen_ut_lockstep_forced_red, whose designed failure is the scoreboard's ISA error and whose red_expect is
# 'UVM_ERROR .*\[isa_[a-z_]+\]'. Sampling earlier must not weaken that, so the fixture was run on the fixed test.
# $ SEED=1 gen_tb_local.sh run ... gen_ut_lockstep +gen_isa_string=rv32imc_zicsr_zifencei   [run name fred1]
#   verdict: FAIL
#   reason: uvm_error at sim.log:32
#   GEN_UT_LOCKSTEP retired 169 consumed 169 mismatches 676 tohost 0x00000001
# $ command grep -a -c 'UVM_ERROR .*\[isa_' $S/l33/l40_root/outA/fred1/sim.log
#   676
# $ command grep -a -m1 -o 'UVM_ERROR .*\[isa_[a-z_]*\]' $S/l33/l40_root/outA/fred1/sim.log
#   UVM_ERROR dv/auto_dv/env/gen_rvfi_pkg.sv(260) @ 142500: uvm_test_top.env.sb [isa_trap]
# $ command grep -a 'GEN_UT_LOCKSTEP retired' <the flow's own run of that entry>/sim_stdout.log
#   GEN_UT_LOCKSTEP retired 171 consumed 171 mismatches 685 tohost 0x00000001
# The fixture fails as designed, with 676 error lines of the class its red_expect names, and its pair reads equal
# (169 and 169), so the failure is the ISA mismatch and not the sample. The flow's own run of that entry, quoted
# just above, reports a MISMATCH COUNT of 685 at 171 retired against my 676 at 169: the earlier sample sees two
# fewer records and nine fewer mismatches, and the entry grades on the line class rather than on any count, which
# is why the difference is harmless. It is stated because a reader comparing the two figures would have to guess.
#
# ================ THE SOURCE-SET DIGESTS, both sides, and the CR-37-L-2 correction ================
# CR-37-L-2 asked for the two tree figures behind landing 38, because that log names one digest and a reader
# recomputing the recipe over the landing's own commit gets another. Both are measured here, and the landing-38
# log stays closed at its committed bytes. The recipe is the local runner's own (gen_tb_local.sh compile): a
# sha256 per file over dv/auto_dv env, tb, isa and gen_tb (sv svh f cc h py yaml sh, no __pycache__), then a
# sha256 over that list, first 16 characters. Tool: $S/l33/l40_digest.sh <root>.
# $ for sha in df1d8c5 9baf3f9 8cc5026 ; do git archive $sha | tar -x -C $S/l33/dig/$sha ; bash $S/l33/l40_digest.sh $S/l33/dig/$sha ; done
#   50981c82f49d7574  files=77  root=archive of df1d8c5
#   034f4e4f2905cb72  files=77  root=archive of 9baf3f9
#   18c7a44b8edfefc4  files=77  root=archive of 8cc5026
# $ bash $S/l33/l40_digest.sh /localdev/fzhang/ws/ibex-challenge
#   a2b799c2a12c9c06  files=77  root=the clone, landing 40 applied
# $ bash $S/l33/l40_digest.sh $S/l33/l40_root
#   a2b799c2a12c9c06  files=77  root=root A after the patch, the set the green ran
# READ THESE AS FOUR DIFFERENT THINGS. 50981c82f49d7574 is the source set at df1d8c5, the tree landing 38 was
# developed on and the one figure that log names. 034f4e4f2905cb72 is the source set at landing 38's own commit
# 9baf3f9, which is what a reader recomputing over that commit gets; neither figure is wrong and the difference is
# the class CR-37-L-2 named. For THIS landing: 18c7a44b8edfefc4 is the source set of root A's base 8cc5026 and so
# the COMPILE-TIME figure of the one simv that served the red and the green; a2b799c2a12c9c06 is the source set
# with the fix applied, and it reads the same in the clone and in root A, so the green ran the bytes this landing
# lands. The simv is shared because only Python changed, the same argument landing 38 made, now with both figures
# named rather than one.
# The pre-fix state of root A is no longer on disk, since the patch tool rewrote it in place, and it does not need
# to be: the red's test bytes are the clone's committed bytes, md5s named above, and the archive of 8cc5026 under
# $S/l33/dig reproduces that source set exactly.
